    "description": "The Edge Gateway maintains a routing configuration containing 20,000 routes and is read by 48 request-handling threads on every request. An administrative reload may replace the configuration several times per minute, but readers must never wait behind a reload and a request must continue seeing one internally consistent version even if another thread publishes a replacement. RoutingSnapshot values are copied into request contexts, so allocating and filling 20,000 routes for every copy would create unacceptable latency and allocation pressure. The implementation needs cheap copies, safe publication of a new version, and retirement of an old version only after its remaining users are finished. The key choice is how to protect both the shared payload and the state that tracks which version is current while keeping the read path nonblocking.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose the internal representation and publication mechanism that provide cheap version copies, nonblocking reads, isolated reloads, and safe reclamation of retired configurations.",
    "notes": "thread-safe-copy-on-write fits the 48-reader rare-reload gateway with cheap snapshot copies and atomic publication; copy-on-write is the near miss for readers that miss the thread-safety requirement, and intrusive-reference-counting supports handle lifetime tracking."
  },
